WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.340
But let me refresh you

2
00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:04.530
and take a little recap of her past

3
00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.930
so we can talk about the present and the future.

4
00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.970
Almost 30 years ago, a shocking incident

5
00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:13.750
rocked the nation and dominated the news.

6
00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.580
In a sleepy Virginia town,

7
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.740
Lorena Bobbitt drew global attention

8
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.010
when she dismembered her husband John's private part

9
00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:22.150
with a kitchen knife

10
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.020
and threw it out the car window,

11
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.990
claiming that he sexually assaulted her.

12
00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:28.820
John denied abusing his wife

13
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.090
and claimed he was an abused husband.

14
00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:32.760
Ultimately, Lorena and John

15
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.900
were both charged with separate crimes,

16
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.220
and then both acquitted.

17
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.800
Painted as a vengeful, unhinged wife,

18
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.350
Lorena was a punchline

19
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:44.010
on late night shows and in headlines,

20
00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.350
but maintained that she was a terrified

21
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.970
and trapped young woman

22
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:51.660
who endured years of alleged domestic abuse

23
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.490
and finally snapped.

24
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.980
Today, Lorena is an advocate

25
00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.940
for domestic abuse survivors.

26
00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.560
She founded the Lorena Gallo Foundation

27
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.970
and create safe spaces for victims,

28
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:06.460
providing the support that she says she needed.

29
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.620
Lorena lives with her longtime partner

30
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.310
and their daughter in that same small Virginia town

31
00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.350
where she became infamous decades ago.

32
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.343
Her story lives on.

33
00:01:18.915 --> 00:01:21.296
(audience applauds)
Ladies and gentlemen,

34
00:01:21.296 --> 00:01:23.639
please welcome Lorena Gallo!
(audience cheering)

35
00:01:23.639 --> 00:01:25.690
Thank you so much.

36
00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.877
Thank you, everybody. Thank you.

37
00:01:27.877 --> 00:01:29.462
Thank you so much.

38
00:01:29.462 --> 00:01:30.295
Thank you.

39
00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:35.883
I mean, I'm freaking out to meet you. I really am.

40
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.100
let me just say one of the things

41
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.080
that I really admire about you is that

42
00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.030
you dismembered your husband,

43
00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.260
you chucked it out the window,

44
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.230
you became like the butt of jokes,

45
00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.050
and when I see you hearing and talking about it,

46
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.240
you have a sense of humor.

47
00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.210
You are in on the joke.

48
00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:01.870
You allow people to giggle or be uncomfortable.

49
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.110
And how do you manage that?

50
00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.350
Because I felt like a punchline too. I was washed up.

51
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.170
They're very different circumstances,

52
00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:15.170
but I oddly relate a bit to the feelings

53
00:02:15.283 --> 00:02:20.283
of how do I ever change this narrative about me?

54
00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.680
Well, it's part of being human too, actually.

55
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.870
I look at my story as this big tree

56
00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:30.730
that turns into branches.

57
00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.420
Yes, we talk about serious issues

58
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.240
like domestic abuse, women's issues,

59
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.560
sexual assault, marital rape,

60
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.760 line:15%
but also has a bizarre twist into my own story,

61
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.220 line:15%
which is the jokes and also the humor in it.

62
00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:49.020 line:15%
And I said to myself, "Well, you know,

63
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.160 line:15%
I am human too and I have to learn to live with.

64
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.600 line:15%
And it's very sad to minimize the horrors

65
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.660
that domestic violence implicates on a person."

66
00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.770
Because it really drains you,

67
00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:07.516
not only mentally, physically, but psychologically.

68
00:03:07.516 --> 00:03:11.530
It really takes a big step on the women's health

69
00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:14.530
and anybody who is a victim of domestic abuse.

70
00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:17.810
So to actually separate myself from that

71
00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:20.500
and became the punch line of jokes

72
00:03:21.395 --> 00:03:24.500
and people making all kinds of, you know,

73
00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.920
I was basically like a cartoon character

74
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.350
that people can't get away from it.

75
00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:34.783
So I have to take

76
00:03:34.783 --> 00:03:39.410
my life back and basically turn it around.

77
00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.650
And I said, "Well, it's part of humans to joke about.

78
00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:47.410
It's part of, you know, we leave with jokes all around."

79
00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:49.530
And I had to understand that.

80
00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.690
And once you understand that part

81
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.940
that comes with the nature of being human,

82
00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.920
then you're basically,

83
00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.910
it's part of the healing process.

84
00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:04.440
It took a long time to get to where I am right now.

85
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.810
It's really psychologically.

86
00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.090
I mean, I was lucky to have doctors

87
00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:12.440
that help me to go through that process

88
00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:15.120
and to start the healing process.

89
00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.210
It took years, I mean,

90
00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:18.070
I can laugh about it and joke about it now,

91
00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:21.040
but really it's not a laughing matter,

92
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.020
you know, the more we talk

93
00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:24.650
and the more we get involved as an advocate

94
00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.270
of domestic abuse, sexual assault,

95
00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:31.270
and marital rape, but I have to create a balance.

96
00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.620
And I'm a keynote speaker

97
00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.010
for colleges and universities.

98
00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:40.700
And when I do that, in order for me to reach out

99
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.040
and to connect with the young generation,

100
00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.940
you have to basically introduce humor to it.

101
00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.300
And my story has that.

102
00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.720
And so, basically, this is my platform

103
00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.550
and I utilize it to help others

104
00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.621
to escape domestic abuse.

105
00:04:59.621 --> 00:05:02.150
I love that, I love that you are able

106
00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:03.810
to humanize and humor.

107
00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.570
I love that you didn't run away.

108
00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.870
You still live in this small town.

109
00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.400
You are a fierce advocate for women.

110
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.873
I mean, that is the opposite of hiding.

111
00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:18.489 line:15%
There's so many layers.

112
00:05:18.489 --> 00:05:21.072 line:15%
(upbeat music)

